<.shell title="Filter bar" current={:filter_bar}>
  <h1>Filter bar</h1>
  <p class="lead">Search and filters above a long list.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.filter_bar
      for={sample_filters()}
      id="filter-bar-demo"
      count="212 activities"
      clear="#"
      path_fn={fn _ -> "#" end}
      page={sample_filter_page()}
      onsubmit="return false"
    >
      <:search field={sample_filters()[:q]} />
      <:filter
        field={sample_filters()[:kind]}
        label="Kind"
        options={sample_kinds()}
        default="all"
      />
      <:filter
        field={sample_filters()[:when]}
        label="When"
        options={sample_whens()}
        default="all"
      />
      <:filter
        field={sample_filters()[:hours]}
        label="Hours"
        options={sample_hours()}
        default="all"
        more
      />
      <:filter
        field={sample_filters()[:status]}
        label="Status"
        options={sample_statuses()}
        default="all"
        more
      />
    </.filter_bar>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    Above a list long enough to page through, often 200 rows or more: activities, members, a member's attendance. Changing a menu updates the list straight away, so there is no button.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A list of a few dozen rows. Leave it plain, or give it a search box and a <a href={
        page_path(:view_switch)
      }>view switch</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sorting. The column headers sort a <a href={page_path(:table)}>table</a>, so there is no sort menu.
    </li>
    <li>
      A form that saves something. Use <a href={page_path(:forms)}>inputs</a> with a Save button.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each menu shows its label inside. A menu not at its default turns blue.</li>
    <li>
      With more than 3 or 4 menus, the row would wrap. Mark the less common ones <code>more</code>: they wait behind a More filters button, and a menu that is on always shows, so the bar never hides what filters the list.
    </li>
    <li>Search waits 300 ms after typing stops.</li>
    <li>
      Under the bar: the count, then Clear filters while a menu filters the list, or Clear search while only the search does, and which rows the page shows.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a phone, the menus fold behind a Filters button that counts the filters that are on. Those filters show as chips under the search box, and selecting one removes it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Lists page by 50, with <a href={page_path(:pagination)}>pagination</a> under the table.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.filter_bar&gt;</code>
    with a <code>:search</code>
    slot and a <code>:filter</code>
    slot per menu, each with its <code>default</code>, and <code>more</code>
    on the ones that can wait behind More filters. A <code>:lead</code>
    slot puts a view switch first. Put a long menu, such as groups, in a
    <code>type="combobox"</code>
    filter. Give the sort to <code>hidden</code>
    so a change keeps it. It is a <code>&lt;.filter_form&gt;</code>, which sends
    <code>change</code>
    on every edit and on Enter. The <a href={page_path(:filter_a_list)}>Filter a list</a>
    pattern shows it with a table.
  </p>
</.shell>
